// THE SURGE — MASHTERPIECE — one song: what it plays, how it is arranged, how it sounds.
//
// THE SURGE as an orchestral-trailer hybrid — the FINAL BOSS of the four Surge remixes.
// A game alternate of surge, selectable from the dev menu (GAME ALTERNATES) and on the
// desk. Nothing replaces the live song.
// 
// It keeps the original's 132 BPM, A minor and syncopated break (x.xx.xx.x.xx.xx.,
// claps ....x..x....x...), so it is the one version the opening film could also use:
// bar 1 slams in on the downbeat with the loop — no fade, no quiet intro — and can be
// the film's door-cut slam.
// 
// The riff is a relentless marcato string ostinato (its rests filled with a pedal that
// walks the bass's A F | G E), the original saw lead doubles it in the drops, a brass
// section states THE THEME (the riff at half speed) as a heroic counter-line, low-brass
// braams mark each chord change in the builds, a choir takes the theme, taiko drums
// fatten the break's 3-3-2 accents, orchestra hits land on them, and celli double a
// Reese and sub on the Surge bass line.
// 
//   1–4    IGNITION — the original loop, slamming; strings and celli join in 3, taiko
//          and orchestra hits in 4 (the cabinet-screen loop, variants.select)
//   5–8    build — braams on Am F G E, the ostinato over taiko; choir swell in 7,
//          timpani roll and riser 7–8
//   9–24   DROP ONE — brass THEME 9–16 (square double); choir THEME 17–24, horns and ice
//          bell with it; the strings never stop
//   25–40  CHANNEL SURF — four glitch-cut windows over the Surge bass and break:
//          25–28 FIELD SERVICE on the chip square, strings in spiccato (crushed kit)
//          29–32 SPEED ZONE on the violins against the saw riff, speed's gallop bass,
//                out on a tape stop
//          33–36 TERMINAL VELOCITY on the alloy chime, violin section, half-time snare
//          37–40 RHYTHM BANKRUPTCY on a bit-crushed square and toy piano, the riff's head
//                answering in its gaps
//   41–48  BREAKDOWN — drums out; THE THEME as a slow string chorale; CRYPT SHIFT on the
//          choir (Am Am F E), then FROST FORTRESS on the ice bell (Dm6 G, frost's bass walk)
//   49–52  build two — taiko roll, braams F G E7 F#7, riser, choir up a step
//   53–68  DROP TWO — B minor, the saw riff an octave up: CARDBOARD KINGDOM on toy piano
//          + ice-bell glockenspiel 53–60 under the brass THEME; CORPORATE KOMBAT as a
//          brass fanfare 61–68 over office's harmony; bar 68 tape-stops back down
//   69–76  EVERYTHING AT ONCE — A minor: the riff, choir + brass THEME, CRYPT SHIFT in the
//          low brass, SPEED ZONE interlocking with the riff; bar 76 glitches into bar 9
// 
// THE EIGHT QUOTES: Field Service 25–28 · Speed 29–32 and 69–76 · Neon 33–36 ·
// Rhythm 37–40 · Crypt 41–44 and 69–76 · Frost 45–48 · Cardboard 53–60 · Office 61–68.
// Each at its own pitch (they all sit in the Surge's key), none rewritten.
// 
// THE LOOP: the Surge riff or THE THEME is in every section outside a quote window, and
// the riff's head answers at every channel-surf seam. The song loops 9–76 (68 bars,
// 124 s), so a clean run rarely hears the loop point; the cabinet screen loops 1–4.
// 
// 76 bars at 132, 138 s. Written by work/local/_surge-remix-mashterpiece.mjs through
// work/local/_remix-lib.mjs and work/local/_surge-quotes.mjs; re-running it rewrites this file.
//
// The music below is the composition. Everything under THE DESK WRITES BELOW HERE
// is written by `npm run mixer` and will be rewritten on every save — put notes
// about the song up here, where they survive.
